# Line endings are CONTENT in this tree, not formatting. Two rules, opposite
# directions, and each one is the fix for a measured defect.
#
#
# 1. EXECUTABLE TEXT MUST BE LINE FEEDS, OR THE KIT DOES NOT RUN AT ALL
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is git's DEFAULT ON WINDOWS, and it converts every text
# file on checkout -- including the shell scripts. A .sh file with carriage
# returns is not a shell script any more; the interpreter reads the return as
# part of the last word on the line:
#
#     $ sh docs/adr/adr-lint.sh
#     docs/adr/adr-lint.sh: line 27: : command not found
#     docs/adr/adr-lint.sh: line 28: set: -: invalid option
#
# Measured on a real clone: TWELVE of twelve executables the kit ships would not
# run. Six linters (adr, prd, link, agents, audit-record, pr-link), the
# discipline-test runner, the two checks run by hand (expect-check,
# provenance-check), and all three git hooks -- including the pre-commit hook
# that is supposed to enforce the gate. The whole quality gate, silent, on the
# platform where the kit is most likely to be adopted cold.
#
# An earlier count here said eleven. It both omitted provenance-check.sh and
# named pr-link-lint twice -- once inside "all six linters" and again as "the
# PR link check". The rule always covered all twelve; only the arithmetic was
# wrong, which is its own small lesson about counting by prose.
#
# This is why the CRLF fixtures below could not have been exercised by the
# adopter they were written for: that adopter never reached the linter.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises the blob to line feeds AND forces line feeds in the
# working tree, whatever core.autocrlf says. It is NOT the repository-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf` rejected below: it is anchored to docs/ and .githooks/,
# the two directories the kit owns, so an adopter's own scripts elsewhere in
# their repository are left entirely alone.
#
# WHAT THIS RULE DOES, AND WHAT THE LINTERS DO. This rule only makes the scripts
# RUNNABLE. Every ordinary Markdown file in the tree still arrives with carriage
# returns, and reading those correctly is each linter's own job -- done on this
# branch for all six, plus expect-check.sh. Measured on a clone, counting the
# seven commands AGENTS.md lists under "Checks you can run": 7 of 7 green, where
# before this branch it was 0 of 7 (nothing ran at all) and after the eol rule
# alone it was 3 of 7.
#
# So the two halves are separable and both are needed. Delete this rule and
# nothing runs; keep it but revert the linters and four of the seven fail on a
# tree they should accept -- agents-lint, audit-record-lint, the discipline
# runner and expect-check.
#
#
# 2. TWO FIXTURES MUST KEEP THEIR CARRIAGE RETURNS
#
# docs/adr/tests/good-crlf/ and docs/links/tests/good-crlf/ are fixtures whose
# files carry Windows line endings. They are tests only while they keep them.
#
# Git normalises a text file to line feeds in the blob and converts on checkout,
# so under `core.autocrlf=input` -- a common setting, and the default on no
# platform, which is what makes it easy to miss -- a clone rewrites these files
# to plain line feeds. Both cases then pass while testing nothing: a green with
# no assertion behind it, which is the class of defect the fixtures exist to
# close. Measured on three throwaway repositories; `eol=crlf` is the only one of
# the three arrangements that survives.
#
# `eol=crlf` keeps the blob as line feeds -- so the diff stays readable and the
# files stay mergeable -- and forces carriage returns in the WORKING TREE on
# every platform, whatever `core.autocrlf` is set to.
#
# Scoped to these two directories on purpose. A repository-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf` would HIDE the defect rather than fix it (a workaround
# under R4), and would impose a line-ending policy on every adopter's checkout,
# which is not this kit's business.
# 1 — executable text. Line feeds, or it will not run.
#
# ANCHORED to the two directories the kit owns. A bare `*.sh` carries no slash,
# so git matches it against the BASENAME AT EVERY DEPTH: it governed every .sh
# file in an adopter's repository, vendored trees included, and on contact it
# marked their CRLF-committed scripts modified. Every executable this kit ships
# is under docs/ or .githooks/, so nothing is lost by saying so.
docs/**/*.sh                  text eol=lf
.githooks/**                  text eol=lf

# 2 — the two fixtures whose carriage returns ARE the test.
#
# If you ever need to pin a path holding a SPACE -- this tree ships two, under
# docs/adr/tests/ and docs/prd/tests/ -- note that gitattributes cannot express
# one. A literal space ends the pattern, so `good-path with space/**` is read as
# the pattern `good-path` plus the attribute names `with` and `space/**`, and
# git reports `is not a valid attribute name` against a token that looks
# unrelated while the path itself silently resolves `unspecified`. Backslash
# escaping and a bracket class both fail the same way. Only the `?` wildcard
# works: `good-path?with?space/**`. Neither spaced fixture needs pinning today
# -- their endings are not the assertion -- so nothing here does this; it is
# written down because the obvious attempt fails quietly.
docs/adr/tests/good-crlf/**   eol=crlf
docs/links/tests/good-crlf/** eol=crlf
